INTRO(3)                       P32W Unix                        INTRO(3)

NAME
       intro - introduction to the C library

SYNOPSIS
       cc -o prog prog.c

DESCRIPTION
       Section 3 describes the functions of the C library,
       /usr/lib/libc.a, other than the system calls, which are in
       section 2. Their declarations are in the headers in
       /usr/include, named on each page's SYNOPSIS.

       cc(1) links every program with the library: it passes ld(1)
       /usr/lib/crt0.o first, then the program's objects, then
       /usr/lib/libc.a, from which ld takes only the members the
       program uses. There are no other libraries (no libm) and no
       -l option. By hand:

           ld -o prog /usr/lib/crt0.o prog.o /usr/lib/libc.a

       crt0.o is the start-up code: the kernel enters it with argc,
       argv and the environment on the stack; it sets environ,
       calls main(argc, argv, envp) and passes main's result to
       exit(3).

       THE LANGUAGE
       cc accepts C89 and much of C99 (cc(1)): char (signed) is 8
       bits, short 16, int, long and pointers 32, long long and
       double 64 (float is double; P32W has no FPU, so doubles are
       softfp(3) underneath, behind the compiler); memory is
       little-endian. The first four
       arguments of a call travel in registers r1-r4, the rest on
       the stack, except that a variadic function receives all of
       them on the stack, so it must be declared before it is
       called (stdarg(3)). cc(1) has the details.

       ERRORS
       A failing system call returns -1 and sets errno; perror(3)
       and strerror print why. Library functions say on their pages
       what they return on failure.

